SYNERGY='''# Team synergy and counterplay — season0-proposed

These are new 2HG adaptations, not tournament-tested pairings. Teams have shared priority and phases (805.5, 805.7, 805.8), but separate objects/resources (810.5). The rules companion contains exact Oracle identities and controller assumptions. No extra team/combo construction fee is charged. Neither point compliance nor these conditional lines grants league gameplay approval.

## A: two distinct routes under shared protection

A1 primarily wins with its own Oracle plus Consultation/Pact. A2 makes arbitrary own mana using Druid/Swift, Tender/Freed, Basalt/Zirda or Kinnan, or Caller/Helix/Crypt/Bird; own command-zone Thrasios draws its own deck and own Ballista finishes. With only G, cast green tutors for a green answer when needed before conversion; don't pretend G pays every white/blue answer. Midgame Tymna can count opponents hit by either teammate; Kraum sees each opposing head’s second spell. Bruse buffs only A2 creatures, while life gained reaches the shared pool. A1’s discard/counters cover engines; A2’s Kutzil/Teferi can protect both heads’ friendly turn. Both heads have Chant, each targeting one opponent, for six combined construction points.

Weaknesses: A1’s premium Oracle package consumes resources that could accelerate development. A2’s dorks expose it to sweepers and creature-ability hate. Torpor Orb stops Oracle but not all A2 mana production; Totem can stop Thrasios/Ballista conversion even if noncreature Swift Druid still taps. Land channels and explicit removal matter. A1 own Tainted Pact requires unique names; its final main deck meets that. Ordinary Cyclonic Rift overload also bounces A2 nonland permanents, so use a targeted bounce unless the reset justifies the friendly losses.

## B: shared combat plus independent Pod

B1 presents Warrior pressure, Demonic Tutor access and combat untap engines; B2 can supply genuine cross-controller untaps from its own connecting creatures. Derevi may target B1 lands or mana creatures. B1 then pays its own WUBRG, and Najeela adds a shared combat. Neither hands nor mana pools merge. The illustrated Bear Umbra line only needs one specific B2 Derevi connection per cycle once four B1 lands and a B1 Bird are configured correctly. Without that setup, a bare Derevi plus Najeela pair is not automatically infinite. Own Najeela tokens entering attacking are not declared attackers for Repository triggers.

B2 retains an independent Preston/Guardian engine and a Finale/Ballista conversion. Pod sacrifices its own creatures only; own Derevi can return through its command-zone ability. Preston copies are 0/1, so a pile of tokens alone is not lethal. B1 also has a same-controller Dualcaster/Twinflame loop: cast Twinflame targeting an own creature, cast Dualcaster copying Twinflame to target own Dualcaster, repeat finite many hasty copies. Combat or own Bombardment converts them; pay the full own 2R+1R and hold appropriate original spell/target. Cross-head split of those two spells is not assumed to self-sustain.

Weaknesses: defenders can deny the critical Derevi connection; killing Najeela before activation or removing Umbra disrupts the team loop. Wipes punish both heads. Orb attacks B2 ETB engines, Totem attacks creature activations, and global taxes can interfere with both. B2 Glen/Vizier counters noncreature spells only and costs U every cycle; it does not counter opposing commander ninjutsu or every creature. The creature combo has a different combat exposure from Najeela, but these are not fully independent hate profiles.

## C: coordinated libraries, independent Ninja triggers

C1 is the leaner tempo/Oracle head; C2 retains high flips, Doomsday and Nanogene multipliers. One Symmetry targets two friendly players and each searches their own library. With one Yuriko connection per head, Draco16 yields 32 opposing shared life loss per reveal, 64 combined. The card is free in this snapshot. The sequence needs both Dracos still available, correct independent UB and B, successful search and unblocked damage. This is a concerning inexpensive strategy, not proof of a likely turn-three win.

C2 Caller permits its own Nanogene after blockers, when small attackers are already unblocked. C2 targets its own Yuriko. C1/C2/enemy creatures can all become copies, but each head controls its own triggers; n own Yuriko copies and m own connecting Ninjas create n*m triggers for that head. Opposing creatures become black copies too, so pre-block conversion makes Cover of Darkness fear inadequate. Own Throne chosen Ninja increases only own other Ninja creature triggers. Infiltrator’s mandatory draw needs deliberate stack ordering to avoid consuming the next planned flip; Deep Hours can be declined. C2 Doomsday is a separate line requiring two own Ninja connections and an appropriate five-card pile; it halves the shared friendly life once. Extra turns are team turns, making C1 Time Warp and C2 Mastery/Trespass offensive team resources.

Weaknesses: both commanders share a name and hence Pithing Needle can stop both commander-ninjutsu abilities. Needle is a relevant opposing card, not secretly added to these submitted lists. Drannith does not prevent ninjutsu; Cursed Totem applies to creature permanents, not Yuriko’s card ability in CZ. Neither Narset nor Notion Thief stops Yuriko putting a revealed card in hand. Remove Ninjas before damage, use damage prevention, counter search/trigger spells or abilities, or disrupt top cards at the right time. Board wipes and repeated commander removal pressure both heads. C2’s large cards impose real opening-hand and casting costs, quantified as card-profile diagnostics rather than fabricated win rates.

## Adversarial matchup probes

These are conditional desk checks with specified resources, not games played or matchup percentages. `stress_test.csv` records them in machine-readable form.

- Against C’s Symmetry, A1 can use its own Force plus another blue card. Alternatively own Aven Mindcensor with 2W and flash restricts opposing searches to four cards; this removes a guarantee, not necessarily every successful Draco search. Remain within actual timing and mana.
- Against A2 Caller/Crypt, B2 Haywire Mite can exile Crypt in response to bounce when it is a noncreature artifact on battlefield, with B2’s own G and creature activation available. Ouphe alone is insufficient: casting Crypt and Caller/Bird activations are not artifact activations. Interact with Caller/Bird, Helix or Crypt instead.
- Against B’s combat loop, C1 can bounce Derevi before damage using Chain of Vapor with own U. B2 can recast/redeploy later, but the specific current combat trigger is denied. If B1 already has a sufficient self-sustaining Nature’s Will/Repository line, removing allied Derevi alone may not suffice.
- Against B2 Pod, A1 Dress Down or a genuine Torpor Orb stops new ETB triggers while active. Dress Down also strips creature abilities and is temporary; plan exact timing. It is not a universal answer to Najeela’s already-resolving ability or all spells.
- All three teams carry land-channel answers on applicable heads. Spell-only Kutzil/Teferi restrictions do not stop activation from hand. A played land is not an available channel card.
- Both B commanders and both C commanders consume zero CZ points; their independently legal combinations are allowed in this test. This is deliberate exposure of the schedule’s free-command-zone risk, not a secret price correction.

No global optimum, actual win percentage, or desired-Bracket-3 certification is claimed. These six complete decks are reproducible strong candidates for organizer review and real table testing.
'''
GOLDFISH='''# Opening hands and goldfish demonstrations

**Possibilities, not observed games or average win turns.** All named cards occur in the submitted head’s own deck. No tutor counts as an extra opening card; tutors named below find the named card from that own library. These sequences assume timely land availability, no opponent successfully intervenes and specified combat connections. Shared team starts at60 under the league’s verified format; resource ownership remains separate. Opening-hand diagnostics later in this document deliberately simulate only random seven-card properties, without mulligans, colored-source solving, fetch sequencing, gameplay decisions or win simulation.

## A1: third-turn Oracle with independently deployed protection

A1 has Tundra, Underground Sea, Watery Grave, Oracle and Consultation by its third shared turn. Three untapped lands produce UUB; cast Oracle UU, then Consultation B with ETB on stack, naming Forest (absent from A1). A2 can deploy Kutzil before this using its own lands/dork; a possible start is Tropical Island/Birds, then Tundra, then Savannah. Kutzil protects both friendly heads from opposing spells on the shared friendly turn, not abilities. A1 has spent all three lands and cannot additionally claim a W Chant. Alternatively, on turn4 use four own sources UUBW and two independent W Chants, one per head targeting one opponent each. Counter Oracle/trigger or remove the protection first; simply killing Oracle does not necessarily prevent the empty-library win.

## A2: third-turn Caller mana, with a specific seven

Own opening seven: Tropical Island, Tundra, Savannah, Birds of Paradise, Valley Floodcaller, Retraction Helix, Tormod’s Crypt. T1 Tropical casts Bird. T2 Tundra: hold both lands plus Bird to flash Caller for2U at the opponent end step; these resources cannot also cast Thrasios on T2. T3 Savannah, Helix U on own ready Caller. Crypt costs0. Tap Caller to bounce own Crypt; tap Bird for any color; recast Crypt, letting Caller’s noncreature-cast trigger untap both own Otter and Bird. Repeat for arbitrary own mana. Cast own Thrasios from command zone, draw own deck, cast own Ballista with sufficient X and shoot. This works because Bird/Caller are ready by T3; a freshly cast ordinary Bird cannot tap immediately without a relevant permission. Intervene on Helix, cast trigger, Caller, Bird, Crypt, or outlet. This seven is demonstrated, not a stated likelihood.

## B: fourth-turn cross-head Bear Umbra combat engine

B1 opening seven: Savannah, Volcanic Island, Tropical Island, Bayou, Birds of Paradise, Demonic Tutor, Fierce Guardianship. T1 Savannah/Bird. T2 Volcanic, cast Najeela2R using two lands plus Bird. T3 Tropical, Tutor1B using Bird B and Savannah generic, find Bear Umbra. T4 Bayou: cast Umbra2GG on own Najeela using Savannah/Bayou GG, Volcanic and Bird generic; leave Tropical available. Attack Najeela: Umbra untaps four own lands. B2’s possible start is Tropical/Noble onT1, Tundra then Derevi GWU onT2, Savannah then Kutzil1GW onT3. OnT4 B2 Derevi must connect. Derevi trigger untaps B1 Bird; B1’s four lands provide G,R,U,B and Bird W. Activate Najeela WUBRG during combat damage before mana empties. Its extra shared combat untaps attacking Najeela, Derevi and tokens. Repeat the Umbra land untap and Derevi Bird untap while combat connections and required permanents survive. The Bird is not attacking; it relies on Derevi to untap each iteration. Opposing blockers, removal, ability counters and prevention make this conditional, not deterministic through resistance. B2 Kutzil can suppress opposing spells but not these other answers.

## B2: fourth-turn Pod route with explicit outlet

Own opening seven: Forest, Tropical Island, Savannah, Tundra, Llanowar Elves, Birthing Pod, Finale of Devastation. T1 Forest/Elf. T2 Tropical: three mana for Pod plus2 Phyrexian life. T3 Savannah: cast Derevi GWU with three lands, Elf pays1 plus2 life to Pod-sacrifice own Derevi3 into Preston4; return commander to CZ. T4 Tundra: use four own mana for Derevi’s1GWU command-zone ability. ETB and Preston copy supply untaps; legendary copy is not kept alongside the original. Pay1 plus2 life to Pod original Derevi3 into Guardian4. Original Guardian’s ETB can blink Forest, resolving before Preston’s copy trigger. Tap returned Forest G; copy Guardian blinks original Guardian. Repeat to generate arbitrary G and0/1 Illusions, then decline the optional blink to stop. Finale X=60 fetches Ballista; Finale’s immediate +60/+60 keeps zero-counter Ballista alive through SBAs. Pay4 repeatedly in this main phase to add actual counters (the ability itself has no sorcery-only restriction), then remove60 to damage an opposing head. Chord X=0 would not make an unbuffed Ballista survive, and Finale pump is not counters. Disruption of ETBs, Pod activation, Preston, Guardian, Finale or Ballista can break the line.

## C: third-turn two-library Draco burst

C1 seven: Underground Sea, Watery Grave, City of Brass, Changeling Outcast, Scheming Symmetry, Force of Will, Dispel. C2 can have its own three suitable lands, Changeling Outcast, Fierce Guardianship, Misdirection and Spellstutter Sprite; choose Underground Sea, Watery Grave and City of Brass as those lands. Both heads T1 Outcast and keep it throughT2. T3 C1 City B casts Symmetry targeting both friendly heads; each finds its own Draco. C1 retains UB from its other two lands, C2 spends own UB. After blockers, each commander-ninjutsus its own Yuriko returning its own unblocked Outcast. Each connecting Yuriko puts own Draco into hand and causes16 loss to each opposing head. Shared opposing life loses32+32=64. C1 Force plus Dispel pitch can protect Symmetry, but C2’s Fierce is not free until its own commander is already controlled: do not count it as free pre-arrival Symmetry protection. Draco in opening hand requires actual Brainstorm/Rack resources instead, and search/trigger/damage disruption remains relevant.

## C2: later alternate routes, without double-spending mana

Doomsday BBB before combat with own Yuriko and own connecting Outcast plus an actual evasion condition can stack Draco16, Blinkmoth Infusion14 and three planned cards. Two own Ninja damage triggers reveal30 total, losing60 opposing shared life. It costs half the shared friendly life once. This line requires two own Ninjas; the ally’s trigger does not consume C2’s library.

For Nanogene, have own Caller and Yuriko already on battlefield. After friendly Outcasts are unblocked, C2 spends3U and targets its own Yuriko. If C1 owns two total creatures and C2 owns three (Caller, Yuriko, Outcast), the resulting counts are2*1=2 and3*1=3 own triggers. Friendly head C1 may have spent its own B on Symmetry first; do not charge that B to C2 or assume C2 can pay Doomsday BBB and Nanogene3U with only four lands. Order lethal top reveals before draw triggers. Opposing creatures also copy; casting before blockers instead destroys the assumed fear advantage against black blockers.
'''
TRADEOFFS='''# Budget tradeoffs — season0-proposed

The frozen schedule is the recalibration proposal with all17 later organizer location overrides. Allocation changes are deckbuilding decisions, not new card-price overrides. All three teams use120 because each retained final premium package has a stated role; this is not a requirement that weaker slots absorb spare points. Zero-price powerful cards are intentionally exploited. There is no claim of a globally solved deckbuilding optimization problem.

## Premium choices and alternatives

- **A1:** Force14 earns its slot as broad early stack interaction with measured blue support. Oracle10 + Consultation10 + Pact8 preserves a compact own-controller win and redundant library access. Tymna9 is guaranteed card economy; Remora6 and Sentinel5 develop it without committing to a second premium win engine. Kraum2 adds blue/red, haste and opposing-spell draw. Bowmasters3, Chant3 and Caverns2 finish the package. Reject the second expensive Breach/ritual/fast-mana suite, premium Silence/Abolisher and additional taxed free counters before cutting the primary engine. The point distribution below shows what is actually paid, not a rationale to call every zero slot weak.
- **A2:** Fierce13 protects creature conversion once a commander is controlled; Thrasios10 is guaranteed outlet; Library7 finds engines with shared-life flexibility but only its controller’s draw access. Kinnan5 remains both accelerator and Basalt option. Chant3 and Zirda3 protect/convert. Pod2/Freed2, Familiar1/Grounds1/Ballista1 support overlapping own-controller routes. Ordinary tutors are not all interchangeable: Green Sun reaches green creatures only, Pod needs own sacrifices and Drift/Muddle transmute at sorcery speed. Dawnwaker declines Force14 rather than starving its engines; A1 remains broad stack cover, not a source of spendable mana.
- **B1:** Tutor15 was preferred to Force14 after blue-density review; it assembles combat converters or a backup while keeping green creatures/Warriors. Fierce13 is free only with own Najeela controlled. Will4, Umbra3, Sword3 and Repository3 give complementary combat-resource mechanisms. Dualcaster3 enables a compact own combo. Bowmasters3/Chant3/Veil2/Swords1/Caverns2 address relevant interaction. Premium Moxes, Sol Ring and Silence were not silently discounted. A2 and B2 can use unpointed current creature engines to replace some mana, but their vulnerability differs from free artifacts.
- **B2:** Force14 and Fierce13 support both heads’ critical turns using B2’s own spells/cards; Remora6 and Sentinel5 sustain them. Cradle5, Kinnan5 and Drannith5 earn cost as own engine land, mana multiplier and asymmetric casting restriction. Drannith does not stop opposing Yuriko’s command-zone activation. Chant3, Pod2, Emiel2, Veil2, Familiar1, Swords1 and Ballista1 remain. Training Grounds1 was cut for zero-point Glen, not because its card price changed. Glen’s actual U-funded counter loop adds interaction; it is not a free answer to everything.
- **C1:** Force14/Fluster5 retain early high-quality interaction. Oracle10/Consultation10 diversify from Ninja combat; Remora6 supplies cards. Time Warp6 is an offensive extra team turn, not just a single head’s second attack. Bowmasters3/Shoal2/Misdirection2/Caverns2 fill the rest. C2 carries the expensive top tutors instead of duplicating them. Conditional Foil/Daze/discard are real costs, not equal-quality free replacements.
- **C2:** Fierce13 is affordable commander-active protection; Vampiric10 and Mystical7 directly establish top cards. Doomsday8 assembles a precise pile. Mastery7/Trespass7 are both realistic high flips and offensive shared extra turns, though miracle/delve have conditions. Shoal2/Misdirection2/Caverns2 and Dig1/Cruise1 round out paid slots. Force14 was forgone because the other head carries it; C2 still retains actual interaction. No assumed extra pregame cards solve opening hands with several expensive flips.

## Functional and price traps exposed

Teferi, Kutzil, Badgermole Cub, Formidable Speaker, Scheming Symmetry, many creature tutors and Nanogene are free here. Cheap Chant3 can protect both enemy heads only with two independent copies/targets across the team (6total), whereas Silence covers both enemy heads with one card but costs more. No global points are added for that synergy. Free Najeela, Derevi and Yuriko produce the most serious command-zone economy. Price compliance therefore does not imply the intended social environment.

Overloaded Rift resets teammate nonlands too. Foil costs two other own hand cards including Island; pitch spells need actual own support. Kraum’s colors do not make every source permanent blue. A teammate’s creature cannot pay your Pod sacrifice or receive your Bruse trigger. Commander-price alternatives are not added to main prices. Full source-list cuts/additions and current source-list price gaps are generated separately, including old banned/incomplete sources rather than presenting them as budget-legal ancestors.
'''

